# Changes to CI, the skill publishing check, its rule data and its tests need
# approval from a code owner. A pull request runs its own copy of each check,
# and the tests run in the same job before the check, so without this a pull
# request could edit a check to pass. The root .gitattributes is owned too,
# because it can change how the published skills are archived. Until the
# repository has a team for skill maintainers, the admins own all of them.
#
# None of this takes effect until branch protection turns on "Require review
# from Code Owners" for main. Today it only auto-requests review, which is why
# the scope below is kept to the paths where that request is worth the noise.
/.github/ @aws/tools-for-devops-agent-admins
/.gitattributes @aws/tools-for-devops-agent-admins
/tests/ @aws/tools-for-devops-agent-admins

# Skills are deliberately NOT owned yet.
#
# Ownership of /skills/ was for publishing review: skills on main are published
# automatically, so a code owner should see one before it ships. That is a real
# requirement, but enforcing it here today buys nothing and costs a lot.
#
#   It enforces nothing. "Require review from Code Owners" is off, so the entry
#   only auto-requests review. Every merge already needs one approving review,
#   and the approving set is every maintainer and admin on the repository, so
#   ownership would narrow who may approve rather than add a gate.
#
#   It costs the admins their inbox. /skills/ matches almost every contribution,
#   and a review request subscribes each member of the team to the whole pull
#   request thread — every comment, push and check result on it, not one
#   notification. Owning the busiest path in the repository for a control that
#   is switched off is how a team learns to ignore review requests.
#
# Re-enable it when the Skills Catalog change is in production and skill
# publishing is a real release path rather than a merge to main. At that point
# the review is gating something, and it should land together with turning on
# "Require review from Code Owners" so the entry enforces instead of only
# notifying. Un-comment the line below and say so in the pull request.
#
# /skills/ @aws/tools-for-devops-agent-admins

# Connector definitions and connectors/releases.yaml, the version production
# serves. Until the repository has a team for connector maintainers, the admins
# own them.
/connectors/ @aws/tools-for-devops-agent-admins
